### Methods: Statistical Analysis (STROBE Compliant)

Baseline demographic and clinical characteristics of the cohort (Total N = {{ n_total | default(0) }}) were summarized across exposure groups. Categorical variables were presented as counts and percentages, and continuous variables were summarized as mean ± SD or median [IQR] according to normality assessed by Shapiro-Wilk and D'Agostino-Pearson tests. Group differences were evaluated using two-sample t-tests, Mann-Whitney U tests, or Pearson's Chi-square tests as appropriate.

Multivariable logistic regression / Cox proportional hazards models were constructed to evaluate the independent association between {{ exposure_name | default("the primary exposure") }} and {{ outcome_name | default("the clinical outcome") }}, adjusting for pre-specified clinical confounders ({{ confounders | default("age, sex, baseline comorbidities") }}). Collinearity was assessed using Variance Inflation Factors (VIF < 5.0). Odds ratios (ORs) or Hazard ratios (HRs) are reported with exact 95% confidence intervals and two-sided P values. Missing data patterns were evaluated via Little's MCAR test; variables with missing values were imputed using chained equations ($M = {{ m_imputations | default(20) }}$). Sensitivity analyses including E-values were conducted to quantify the robustness of findings against potential unmeasured confounding.
